#--: What a native unit is

A declaration marked #~"is native" has no body in zerolang. Its body is
written in C, in the #_*"C implementation file" of its unit:
#~"<unit>/nativec.zd", in the directory of the unit's own name beside
#~"<unit>.z". A unit with such a declaration is a #_*"native unit". The file
can hold the C for functions, for a class's methods, and for whole classes.
It also holds what the unit needs from the C toolchain: the libraries to link,
the C sources to build, and the compilers that cannot build it.

The file is a data file (see [#> to=./zdata.html: zdata]). Its value has the
type #~"runtime.nativec", which #~"lib/system/runtime.zd" declares. That file
also documents each field. The standard library's units are native units in
the same way: the C of #~"io" is in #~"lib/system/io/nativec.zd".

#ul:
  [#*: The standard library's files are built into #~"zc". A build does not
    read them.]
  [#*: The file of any other unit is read and checked each time a program that
    reaches the unit is built, as #~"zc data" checks a data file. An error in
    the file stops the build, and names the file and the line.]
  [#*: A native declaration that the program uses, and that has no row in its
    unit's file, is error #~"E0501". When the unit has no file, the error names
    the file that it looked for.]

#--: A first native unit

The unit #~"counter" declares two native functions:

#code language=zerolang check=none: """
  # counter.z -- two natives, implemented in counter/nativec.zd

  # triple -- three times n
  triple: function {n: i64} out i64 is native

  # hypot -- the length of the hypotenuse of a right triangle with sides a and b
  hypot: function {a: f64 b: f64} out f64 is native

  main: function is {
      print "\{triple n: 14}"
      print "\{hypot a: 3.0 b: 4.0}"
  }
  """

Its C is in #~"counter/nativec.zd". Each native has a #_*"row", found by its
#~"key": the name of the declaration in its unit. The row's fragment is C that
is written once into a program that uses the native. The C function of a
native free function is named #~"z_<unit>_<name>", and takes its arguments in
their declared order. #~"hypot" calls #~"sqrt", so a program that reaches the
unit links the C math library (#~"m"):

#code language=zdata: """"
  # nativec -- counter's C implementation
  c: runtime.nativec

  # implementation -- the C behind counter's native declarations
  implementation: (
      c.implementation
      rows: list {
          (
              c.row
              key: "triple"
              fragments: list {
                  """
                  static int64_t z_counter_triple(int64_t n) {
                      return n * 3;
                  }
                  """
              }
          )
          (
              c.row
              key: "hypot"
              fragments: list {
                  """
                  static double z_counter_hypot(double a, double b) {
                      return sqrt(a * a + b * b);
                  }
                  """
              }
          )
      }
      links: list { (c.link library: "m") }
  )
  """"

#code: """
  $ zc run counter.z
  42
  5
  """

The fixtures under #~"tests/fixtures/user_native/" are two larger native
units. #~"mystery" has a hidden subunit's native, a fragment that calls the
standard library's C, and a variant row chosen by a fact. #~"cellar" has
native classes written from C templates.

#--: Rows

A row's #~"key" says what the row implements:

#ul:
  [#*: #~"<function>" -- a free function of the unit (#~"readText").]
  [#*: #~"<type>.<member>" -- a member of a type of the unit (#~"File.close",
    #~"u8.+").]
  [#*: #~"@<name>" -- a helper, which several rows share. No declaration of the
    unit can spell a name that starts with #~"@".]

A row holds C in one of three forms:

#ul:
  [#*: #~"body" -- a C expression, written where the native is used. A body
    is for a member of a type: an operator, a conversion, a method. Its holes
    (below) are the operands.]
  [#*: #~"fragments" -- C functions, each written once into a program that
    uses the native.]
  [#*: #~"pieces" -- a fragment in parts. A piece is written only when a need
    names its demand; a piece with no demand is written always.]

The other fields of a row:

#table:
  [#tr: [#th: Field] [#th: What it says]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"needs"] [#td: the rows that this row's C calls. A need is
    a demand of the row's own unit (#~"@errno") or #~"<unit>.<demand>" of
    another unit's (#~"prelude.@xmalloc"). Without the need, the callee is not
    written, and the C does not compile.]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"demand"] [#td: the demands that reach the row, when that is
    not the last part of the key: a member's name, #~"@unit" (the unit is
    used), #~"uses_<T>" (the C names the unit's type #~"T").]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"form"] [#td: what the body is: #~"c.form.call", a call
    (the default), or #~"c.form.value", a value computed from the holes with
    no call.]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"byPointer"] [#td: the body takes its operands by pointer.]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"constant"] [#td: a variant row, used in place of the plain
    row of the same key when the arguments at these positions are constants.]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"fact"] [#td: a variant row, used in place of the plain row of
    the same key when the fact holds (below).]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"pulls"] [#td: the demands of other units that using the row
    makes at once.]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"members"] [#td: the template members of other instances
    that the fragments call (#~"List_String.append").]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"via"] [#td: on a native free function: the template function
    of the class instance that the function returns is its body.]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"callsEquals"] [#td: on an #~"=="/#~"!=" row: the body calls
    the generated #~"==" of its type.]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"call"] [#td: on a #~"StringView" member: how a call of the
    member is written.]]

The rows of a unit are in #_*"load order": the C of a row is written after
the C of the rows above it. A row must stand below every row whose C it uses.

#--: Holes

A body, a fragment and a piece can have #_*"holes": names between #~"@"
characters, which #~"zc" fills when it writes the C.

#ul:
  [#*: #~"@L@", #~"@R@" -- the left and right operands of an operator.]
  [#*: #~"@V@" -- the receiver of a row that takes no argument.]
  [#*: #~"@A1@", #~"@A2@", ... -- the arguments, in declared order. The
    receiver is not counted.]
  [#*: #~"@T@" -- the C name of the receiver's type, without its #~"z_".]
  [#*: #~"@<Type>@" -- the C name of a type, without its #~"z_", by its
    name (#~"z_@File@_t").]
  [#*: #~"@TAG_<type>@", #~"@ARM_<type>.<arm>@", #~"@MEMBER_<type>.<name>@"
    -- the discriminator of a variant, the tag of one of its arms, and a member
    of a struct that #~"zc" writes for a declaration.]

An operand is read once, in written order, before the body. A lone #~"@"
opens a hole, so C that needs the character itself cannot have one in a
fragment.

#--: Facts and variant rows

A #_*"fact" is true of the whole program: it holds when the program uses any
of the declarations it lists. A row with #~"fact" is a variant of the plain
row of the same key. When the fact holds, the variant is used. When it does
not hold, the variant is dropped. The variant stands above the plain row.

#code language=zdata: """
  c: runtime.nativec

  implementation: (
      c.implementation
      rows: list {
          (c.row key: "conjure" fact: "brewing" fragments: list { "/* while brewing */" })
          (c.row key: "conjure" fragments: list { "/* otherwise */" })
      }
      facts: list { (c.fact name: "brewing" reach: list { "mystery.spell.brew" }) }
  )
  """

A link and a source can also hold under a fact. The standard library uses the
fact #~"threaded", which holds when a program starts a thread: then its rows
take locks and its C sources are built thread-safe.

#--: Classes and templates

A class marked #~"is native" has its struct and methods written in C. The
file's #~"classes" list holds one entry for each class whose C struct the
runtime writes: its #~"key" is the class's name (#~"File"), and it can set
these fields:

#table:
  [#tr: [#th: Field] [#th: What it holds]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"template"] [#td: the template the class is written from.]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"live"] [#td: the instances that must all exist for the class
    to be in use.]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"destroy"] [#td: the C of the class's destructor, for what its
    declaration cannot show (a file descriptor, a handle).]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"needs"] [#td: what the class's C calls, as a row's needs.]]

A class's #~"template" names one of the file's #~"templates". A template is C
text with holes of its own: #~"@@NAME@@" is the C name of
the instance, and for each type parameter #~"P", #~"@@P@@" is the C type of its
argument and #~"@@P_DESTROY@@" a function that destroys one. A generic class
is written once for each instance. A class that is not generic is written
once. A template's #~"sections" can each belong to a member: a section is
written only when its member is used. A section of a template the compiler
fills itself may also name one of its type keys as #~"reached" (#~"MAPENTRY"):
it is written only where the program reaches that type as well. Each native method
still has its row.

A native method is a C function #~"z_<class>_<method>", called with a pointer
to the receiver, then the arguments. A view (#~"StringView", a #~".view") is
passed by value, an owned collection by pointer, and anything else by value. A
template defines it as #~"z_@@NAME@@_<method>". A method row of a class that
has no template defines it in a fragment, with the class's C name as a hole:
#~"z_@File@_close(z_@File@_t* p)".

#--: Toolchain requirements

#ul:
  [#*: #~"links" -- a library to link (#~"(c.link library: \"pthread\")").]
  [#*: #~"sources" -- a C source under the vendor directory to compile and
    link, with its #~"defines". The driver builds it into its object cache
    (see #~"zc cobj").]
  [#*: #~"refusals" -- what cannot build the unit: the C compilers that lack
    a feature, or the only architectures that have it. A refused toolchain is
    named before any C is compiled.]

Each can be for one hidden subunit (#~"subunit"), for some operating systems
only (#~"os"), or only when a fact holds (#~"fact"). A requirement applies
only when its unit is reached: a program that does not use the unit does not
link its library.

#--: Threads

A native's C must hold with other threads running, because the checker lets
values move between threads and lets several threads read one value:

#ul:
  [#*: A method with a #~".view" receiver gets a const pointer, and writes
    nothing that its object reaches. Then any number of threads can read one
    object at the same time.]
  [#*: What two values share changes only atomically, or under a lock of its
    own.]
  [#*: State of the whole process is written before the program's #~"main"
    runs, or by a row that holds under the fact #~"threaded".]
  [#*: Use the reentrant C library calls (#~"getpwuid_r", not
    #~"strerror").]

#--: Finding what wrote a line of C

#~"zc emit --mark-natives" writes a comment line before each part of the C
that a native unit's file gave: a row's fragment, a piece, a template, or one
of the fixed files that every program's C starts with. The comment names the
row and the file and line it is written at:

#code: """
  $ zc emit counter.z --mark-natives -o counter.c
  $ grep -A3 'nativec: row counter.triple' counter.c
  /* nativec: row counter.triple, counter/nativec.zd:9 */
  static int64_t z_counter_triple(int64_t n) {
      return n * 3;
  }
  """

Without the comment lines, the C is the same as without the flag.

#~"zc natives show" prints what #~"zc" has built in for the standard
library: the rows of a unit (#~"zc natives show io"), one row with every
variant of it, its fields and its C (#~"zc natives show io.eprintln"), or a
template (#~"zc natives show List"). Each comes with the file and line that
a mark names.

#~"zc natives check rows counter" checks a unit's own file the way the
standard library's are checked, #~"counter" being the unit's folder that
holds its #~"nativec.zd": every native function of the unit has a row, every
row's key names a declaration of the unit, and every hole a text spells names
a type, arm or member that exists. A mistyped key is found here; a build
reports only the declaration it leaves without a row.
